import{_ as s,o as t,c as o,ao as n}from"./chunks/framework.D7TN4pt9.js";const u=JSON.parse('{"title":"Backend & Sessions","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"design/backend.md","filePath":"design/backend.md","lastUpdated":null}'),a={name:"design/backend.md"};function r(i,e,d,c,l,h){return t(),o("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[n(`<h1 id="Backend-and-Sessions" tabindex="-1">Backend &amp; Sessions <a class="header-anchor" href="#Backend-and-Sessions" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Backend &amp; Sessions {#Backend-and-Sessions}&quot;">​</a></h1><div class="tip custom-block"><p class="custom-block-title">Status: partly built</p><p>The daemon, its per-row Julia kernel and REPL, and the socket transport are built. An in-app orchestrator LLM session is not built (see <a href="./requirements">Requirements</a>), and the daemon starts no tmux sessions.</p></div><p>The backend daemon owns project state. It is the only process the frontend talks to over the <a href="./protocol">line protocol</a>, and it sits between the frontend and the Julia kernels it supervises. For the big picture and the reasoning behind the language split, read <a href="./../guide/architecture">Architecture at a Glance</a>.</p><h2 id="What-the-daemon-owns" tabindex="-1">What the daemon owns <a class="header-anchor" href="#What-the-daemon-owns" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What the daemon owns {#What-the-daemon-owns}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A single long-lived Rust process (tokio) holds the row registry, the file watchers and the kernel and REPL children:</p><ul><li><p><strong>Rows</strong> — the registry of rows (workspaces), each with its project directory, its kernel and REPL, and its file watcher.</p></li><li><p><strong>File watching</strong> — a <code>notify</code> watcher per workspace turns external edits into <code>preview.changed</code> events (<a href="./protocol">protocol</a>).</p></li><li><p><strong>Process supervision</strong> — it spawns and supervises each row&#39;s Julia kernel and REPL, restarting the kernel on crash and surfacing failures as protocol events.</p></li></ul><p>The frontend never touches kernel state directly, even when everything runs on one laptop. Local-only operation is &quot;a client connecting to the local daemon&#39;s socket,&quot; not a separate code path.</p><h2 id="Kernel-launch-and-supervision" tabindex="-1">Kernel launch and supervision <a class="header-anchor" href="#Kernel-launch-and-supervision" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Kernel launch and supervision {#Kernel-launch-and-supervision}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The daemon spawns the kernel as a child process and frames the conversation over its stdio, per the <a href="./protocol">line protocol</a>:</p><div class="language-bash vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">bash</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">julia</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> --project=</span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">&lt;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">repo</span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">&gt;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">/julia/kernel</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> --startup-file=no</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> \\</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">  -e</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> &#39;using ShipToolsKernel; ShipToolsKernel.serve(stdin, stdout; project_root=&quot;&lt;row root&gt;&quot;)&#39;</span></span></code></pre></div><p>Spawning is <code>tokio::process::Command</code> through <code>lifecycle::child_signal::Signal::spawn</code>, which puts the child in its own process group (Unix) or job (Windows). Each stderr line goes to the daemon log at debug, and a generation that fails logs the child&#39;s last 30 stderr lines at warn — the <em>only</em> place kernel stderr surfaces, so user-facing errors must travel as protocol events, not prints.</p><p>Restart policy differs by process:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:right;">Process</th><th style="text-align:right;">On exit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="text-align:right;">Kernel</td><td style="text-align:right;">auto-restart, backoff from 250 ms doubling to 30 s, started over only after a kernel that served a minute after its hello; callers get <code>Dead</code> meanwhile; state rebuilds from disk, so restart is safe</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">REPL</td><td style="text-align:right;">not restarted in the background: its state is shown as <code>dead</code>, and the next eval starts a new REPL, which says why the old one ended</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Orphaned <code>julia</code> processes are unacceptable, so the whole tree of every process the daemon starts, but those ADR 0050 names as outside it, is killed (SIGKILL to its process group on Unix, <code>TerminateJobObject</code> on Windows) when its owner lets go of it or the daemon&#39;s controlled end fires the child signal, whichever comes first (ADR 0050, &quot;controlled exits&quot;). The tree is killed before its leader is reaped, because the leader&#39;s pid is the group&#39;s number. There is no SIGTERM grace and no <code>taskkill</code>. A process that deliberately left, for example a Unix descendant that called <code>setsid</code> or <code>setpgid</code>, is the one exception (ADR 0050, residual 7).</p><h2 id="Transport,-persistence,-reconnect" tabindex="-1">Transport, persistence, reconnect <a class="header-anchor" href="#Transport,-persistence,-reconnect" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Transport, persistence, reconnect {#Transport,-persistence,-reconnect}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A common workflow is remote: the window runs on one computer and reaches a daemon on another, where the kernel and GPU live. Local operation is the fall-through. The session must survive SSH disconnects, reconnect from a fresh client, and not depend on host-specific port allocation.</p><ul><li><p><strong>Backend as a user service on the remote.</strong> The daemon runs as the <code>systemd --user</code> unit <code>sotd.service</code> (installed by the release installer), so its lifecycle survives SSH drops.</p></li><li><p><strong>A per-user local socket, not a TCP port.</strong> On Unix it listens at <code>$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/sot/sessions/&lt;label&gt;.sock</code> (a private runtime folder; <code>sot</code> is the label of the persistent daemon), on Windows at a named pipe. Cross-machine, the frontend reaches it by spawning its own <code>ssh</code> child rather than forwarding a port — see <a href="./../concepts/transport">Transport</a>.</p></li><li><p><strong>Transport auth.</strong> The daemon has no TCP listener and no app-level token. The socket&#39;s folder is private to the user, the daemon checks the connecting process&#39;s OS identity when it accepts, and cross-machine access is <code>ssh</code>.</p></li><li><p><strong>Reconnect carries revision.</strong> Every connect sends <code>{session_id, client_id, last_seen_revision}</code>. The backend replays missed events from a bounded ring or marks a full snapshot pending if the client is too far behind. Each connection pings every 30 s, and the daemon reaps one that has been silent for 90 s.</p></li></ul><h2 id="Workspaces" tabindex="-1">Workspaces <a class="header-anchor" href="#Workspaces" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Workspaces {#Workspaces}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>One daemon holds many rows (workspaces), reached over one socket and one <code>ssh</code> child. Each row has its own Julia kernel and its own persistent REPL, and the daemon routes an op to them by <code>workspace_id</code>.</p><ul><li><p>The kernel is a direct child of the daemon, framed over stdio. It runs the daemon&#39;s own <code>julia/kernel</code> project and reads the row&#39;s sources statically; it never runs user code and never loads the user&#39;s environment.</p></li><li><p>User code runs in the row&#39;s REPL, also a daemon child. The REPL activates the row&#39;s project directory as its environment, so two rows can use different <code>Project.toml</code> environments (<code>MyPackage@0.1</code> in one, <code>@0.2</code> in another) in separate Julia processes. Variables live in the REPL.</p></li><li><p><strong>Switching is a frontend state change.</strong> There is no <code>workspace.switch</code> op: the window sets its active workspace, tells the daemon with <code>workspace.activate</code>, and re-fires <code>tree.root</code> with the new id. Nothing restarts, so a REPL holding <code>x = 5</code> still holds it when you return to its row.</p></li><li><p><strong><code>workspace_id</code> is optional on tree, preview, kernel, REPL, concept and pty ops</strong>; a missing field resolves to the default workspace. Lifecycle ops are <code>workspace.list</code>, <code>workspace.create</code> and <code>workspace.destroy</code>.</p></li></ul><p>The daemon owns its kernel and REPL children, so ending the daemon ends them and a REPL&#39;s variables go with it. The rows themselves, each running its agent under a capsule supervisor, outlive the daemon (see <a href="./../concepts/sessions">Sessions and persistence</a>).</p>`,21)])])}const k=s(a,[["render",r]]);export{u as __pageData,k as default};
